<p align="center">These are my favorite books on Zen, all
inexpensive and readily available at <a
href="http://www.amazon.com/" target="_parent">Amazon Books</a>.</p>

<table border="0" width="572">
    <tr>
        <td valign="top" width="77"><img src="b1.jpg" width="77"
        height="120"></td>
        <td valign="top" width="190"><font size="3" face="Arial"><strong>Zen
        in the Art of Archery</strong></font><font size="1"><br>
        by Eugen Herrigel<br>
        </font><font size="2"><br>
        The first Western book on Zen, it was written by a German
        philosopher who studied Zen through the art of Kyudo.
        Almost all recent Western writings on Zen refer to it.</font></td>
        <td valign="top" width="77"><img src="b3.jpg" width="78"
        height="120"></td>
        <td valign="top" width="190"><font size="3" face="Arial"><strong>Illuminated
        Spirit</strong></font><font size="1"><br>
        Conversations with a Kyudo Master<br>
        By Dan and Jackie DeProspero<br>
        </font><font size="2"><br>
        The authors discuss all aspects of their studies under a
        Kyudo master in Japan. This is an outstanding practical
        book on Zen.</font></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td valign="top" width="77"><img src="b2.jpg" width="78"
        height="120"></td>
        <td valign="top" width="190"><font size="3" face="Arial"><strong>One
        Arrow, One Life</strong></font><font size="1"><br>
        Zen, Archery, Enlightenment<br>
        by Kenneth Kushner<br>
        </font><font size="2"><br>
        A tremendous recent book that explores the discoveries of
        the author as he progresses in Zen through his practice
        of Kyudo.</font></td>
        <td valign="top" width="77"><img src="b4.jpg" width="78"
        height="120"></td>
        <td valign="top" width="190"><font size="3" face="Arial"><strong>Zen
        Action, Zen Person</strong></font><font size="1"><br>
        By T. P. Kasulis<br>
        </font><font size="2"><br>
        A very thorough examination of Zen practice and teaching.
        It is the first book on Zen to be written by an American
        philosopher and deeply dissects Zen for a Western
        audience.<br>
        </font></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td valign="top" width="77"><img src="b5.jpg" width="78"
        height="120"></td>
        <td valign="top" width="190"><font size="3" face="Arial"><strong>Zen
        Mind, Beginner's Mind</strong></font><font size="1"><br>
        By Shunryu Suzuki<br>
        </font><font size="2"><br>
        An incredible collection of images and explanations of
        Zen teachings by a gifted and accomplished Zen master who
        established Zen in America in the 1970's.</font></td>
        <td valign="top" width="77"><img src="b6.jpg" width="78"
        height="120"></td>
        <td valign="top" width="190"><font size="3" face="Arial"><strong>Samadhi</strong></font><font
        size="1"><br>
        Self Development in Zen, Swordmanship and Psychotherapy<br>
        By Mike Sayama<br>
        </font><font size="2"><br>
        A superb work on Zen in practice written by a PhD in
        psychology.</font></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td valign="top" width="77"><img src="b7.jpg" width="78"
        height="120"></td>
        <td valign="top" width="190"><font size="3" face="Arial"><strong>Zen
        and the Art of Anything</strong></font><font size="1"><br>
        By Hal French<br>
        </font><font size="2"><br>
        An easy-reading book that uses imagery and quotations to
        explain Zen in everyday life. </font></td>
        <td valign="top" width="77"><img src="b8.jpg" width="80"
        height="120"></td>
        <td valign="top" width="190"><font size="3" face="Arial"><strong>Nothing
        Special</strong></font><font size="1"> </font><font
        size="3"><strong>- </strong></font><font size="3"
        face="Arial"><strong>Living Zen</strong></font><font
        size="1"><br>
        By Charlotte Joko Beck and Steve Smith<br>
        </font><font size="2"><br>
        A quality work by an American Zen teacher that discusses
        solutions in Zen to the many mysteries and difficulties
        of the human experience. </font></td>
    </tr>
